WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:02.770
Our leaf spotter kept getting it wrong.

2
00:00:03.450 --> 00:00:05.650
The whole school had labelled its

3
00:00:05.650 --> 00:00:08.230
photos, and a few labels were flipped.

4
00:00:09.010 --> 00:00:10.770
One student had done it on

5
00:00:10.770 --> 00:00:11.930
purpose, as a joke.

6
00:00:13.190 --> 00:00:15.930
Today: how poisoned data breaks a

7
00:00:15.930 --> 00:00:17.790
model, and how to find it with a

8
00:00:17.790 --> 00:00:18.670
few lines of Python.

9
00:00:20.020 --> 00:00:22.440
A model learns from its examples, so

10
00:00:22.440 --> 00:00:24.660
whoever touches the data can steer it.

11
00:00:25.210 --> 00:00:28.410
Poisoning means slipping in wrong or

12
00:00:28.410 --> 00:00:31.910
planted examples: flipped labels, fake

13
00:00:31.910 --> 00:00:34.170
reviews, edited web pages.

14
00:00:34.920 --> 00:00:36.840
Nobody has to touch the model itself.

15
00:00:38.390 --> 00:00:40.610
A sneakier kind is a backdoor.

16
00:00:41.290 --> 00:00:44.230
The attacker plants a trigger, like a

17
00:00:44.230 --> 00:00:47.470
small sticker or a rare word, and

18
00:00:47.470 --> 00:00:49.510
labels those examples their way.

19
00:00:50.060 --> 00:00:52.860
The model passes every normal test, and

20
00:00:52.860 --> 00:00:55.400
misbehaves only when the trigger appears.

21
00:00:56.770 --> 00:00:59.090
So how do you find poison?

22
00:00:59.850 --> 00:01:01.370
Look at each example's neighbours.

23
00:01:02.170 --> 00:01:04.670
A leaf labelled sick, with healthy

24
00:01:04.670 --> 00:01:07.450
leaves on both sides, is suspicious.

25
00:01:08.500 --> 00:01:11.020
The lab does the same: points whose

26
00:01:11.020 --> 00:01:13.160
neighbours disagree get flagged.

27
00:01:14.230 --> 00:01:15.270
Here it is in Python.

28
00:01:16.110 --> 00:01:17.550
The labels are in order of

29
00:01:17.550 --> 00:01:19.170
how yellow each leaf is.

30
00:01:19.820 --> 00:01:22.320
For each one, compare it with the leaf

31
00:01:22.320 --> 00:01:24.480
on its left and on its right.

32
00:01:25.120 --> 00:01:27.100
If both disagree, flag it

33
00:01:27.100 --> 00:01:28.040
for a person to check.

34
00:01:29.280 --> 00:01:29.860
Quick check!

35
00:01:30.650 --> 00:01:33.830
In order, the labels read: healthy,

36
00:01:34.470 --> 00:01:38.030
healthy, sick, healthy, healthy.

37
00:01:38.810 --> 00:01:40.290
Which one gets flagged?

38
00:01:41.420 --> 00:01:42.800
The sick one in the middle:

39
00:01:43.400 --> 00:01:44.580
both neighbours disagree.

40
00:01:45.410 --> 00:01:45.970
But careful.

41
00:01:46.840 --> 00:01:48.820
Honest examples at the edge of a

42
00:01:48.820 --> 00:01:50.540
group get flagged sometimes too.

43
00:01:51.150 --> 00:01:54.310
So code only flags; a person looks,

44
00:01:54.730 --> 00:01:57.210
decides, and writes down what was removed.

45
00:01:58.500 --> 00:02:00.680
Other defences stop poison getting in.

46
00:02:01.250 --> 00:02:02.870
Know where your data came from.

47
00:02:03.540 --> 00:02:05.460
Let three people label each

48
00:02:05.460 --> 00:02:07.400
example, and keep the majority.

49
00:02:08.120 --> 00:02:10.640
And keep a clean test set nobody

50
00:02:10.640 --> 00:02:12.500
else can touch: if its score

51
00:02:12.500 --> 00:02:14.720
suddenly drops, something got in.

52
00:02:15.880 --> 00:02:18.460
In the app, flip some labels and train:

53
00:02:18.740 --> 00:02:21.460
the line moves and accuracy falls.

54
00:02:22.280 --> 00:02:25.480
Press “Find the odd ones out”, see how

55
00:02:25.480 --> 00:02:27.240
many flagged points were really

56
00:02:27.240 --> 00:02:29.920
poisoned, then drop them and retrain.

57
00:02:31.120 --> 00:02:31.880
Your turn.

58
00:02:32.500 --> 00:02:35.380
New data was added last night, and this

59
00:02:35.380 --> 00:02:38.020
morning the score on your locked test set

60
00:02:38.020 --> 00:02:40.520
fell from ninety-six to eighty.

61
00:02:41.210 --> 00:02:42.470
What do you do first?

62
00:02:43.830 --> 00:02:45.970
Roll back and check the new data:

63
00:02:46.450 --> 00:02:47.990
the drop is your warning.

64
00:02:48.580 --> 00:02:50.580
Remember: whoever touches the

65
00:02:50.580 --> 00:02:52.380
data can steer the model.

66
00:02:52.980 --> 00:02:54.800
Backdoors hide until

67
00:02:54.800 --> 00:02:55.820
their trigger appears.

68
00:02:56.750 --> 00:02:58.510
Code flags odd examples,

69
00:02:58.890 --> 00:02:59.990
and a person decides.

70
00:03:01.450 --> 00:03:03.490
Try it tomorrow: with three

71
00:03:03.490 --> 00:03:05.650
friends, label the same ten

72
00:03:05.650 --> 00:03:07.990
pictures on your own, then compare.

73
00:03:08.790 --> 00:03:10.830
Where you disagree, talk it

74
00:03:10.830 --> 00:03:12.510
through and keep the majority.

75
00:03:13.180 --> 00:03:14.980
That's how real datasets catch

76
00:03:14.980 --> 00:03:16.820
careless and planted labels.
